<setup>

  <selectFromSequence name="p" from="-3" to="3" step="1" exclude="-1 0 1"/>
  
  <function name="f">$p*sin(x)</function>
  <function name="fP">$p*cos(x)</function>
  <function name="fPP">-$p*sin(x)</function>
  <function name="fPPP">-$p*cos(x)</function>
  <function name="fP4">$p*sin(x)</function>
  
  <math name="c0">$$f($a)</math>
  <math name="c1">$$fP($a)</math>
  <math name="c2">$$fPP($a)/2</math>
  <math name="c3">$$fPPP($a)/3!</math>
  <math name="c4">$$fP4($a)/4!</math>
  
<!--
  <selectFromSequence name="c0" from="-2" to="2" step="1" exclude="0"/>
  <selectFromSequence name="c1" from="-3" to="3" step="1" exclude="0 $c0"/>
  <selectFromSequence name="c2" from="-3" to="3" step="1" exclude="0 $c0 $c1"/>
  <selectFromSequence name="c3" from="-3" to="3" step="1" exclude="0 $c0 $c1 $c2"/>
  <selectFromSequence name="c4" from="-3" to="3" step="1" exclude="0 $c0 $c1 $c2 $c3"/>
-->
  
  <selectFromSequence name="pm" from="-1" to="1" step="2"/>
  <selectFromSequence name="k" from="0.2" to="0.4" step="0.1"/>

  <math simplify name="a">$pm*pi/2</math>
  <math simplify name="aDec">$pm*3.14159265359/2</math>
  <math simplify name="h">$a + $k</math>
  <math simplify name="hDec">$aDec + $k</math>
  
  <function name="T4">$c0 + $c1*(x-$a) + $c2*(x-$a)^2 + $c3*(x-$a)^3 + $c4*(x-$a)^4</function>
  <function name="T4Symb">c_0 + c_1*(x-$a) + c_2*(x-$a)^2 + c_3*(x-$a)^3 + c_4*(x-$a)^4</function>
  
  <function name="T3">$c0 + $c1*(x-$a) + $c2*(x-$a)^2 + $c3*(x-$a)^3</function>
  <function name="T3Symb">c_0 + c_1*(x-$a) + c_2*(x-$a)^2 + c_3*(x-$a)^3</function>

  <function name="T2">$c0 + $c1*(x-$a) + $c2*(x-$a)^2</function>
  <function name="T2Symb">c_0 + c_1*(x-$a) + c_2*(x-$a)^2</function>

  <function name="T1">$c0 + $c1*(x-$a)</function>
  <function name="T1Symb">c_0 + c_1*(x-$a)</function>
  
</setup>

<p>
  Consider the function <m>f(x) = $f</m>, suppose we want to use Taylor polynomials to approximate the value of <m>$f</m> near <m>a = $a</m>.
</p>

<!--
<p>
  <m>pm = $pm</m>, <m>k = $k</m>
</p>
-->

<p>
  <ol marker="a.">
    <li>
      <p>
        Find all of the needed derivatives of <m>f(x) = $f</m> as well as their respective values at <m>a = $a</m>.  Report your results for <m>f^{(4)}(x)</m> and <m>f^{(4)}($a)</m> below; make sure the value of <m>f^{(4)}($a)</m> is exact.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>f^{(4)}(x) = </m><answer>$fP4</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>f^{(4)}($a) = </m><answer>$$fP4($a)</answer>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
          Find and state each of the Taylor polynomials of <m>f</m> that are centered at <m>a = $a</m>: <m>T_1(x)</m>, <m>\ldots</m>, <m>T_4(x)</m>.  
      </p>    
      <p>
        <m>T_1(x) = </m><answer name="T1Entered">$T1</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>T_2(x) = </m><answer name="T2Entered">$T2</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>T_3(x) = </m><answer name="T3Entered">$T3</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>T_4(x) = </m><answer name="T4Entered">$T4</answer>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Use <m>T_4(x)</m> to estimate the value of <m>$p \sin($a + $k)</m>.  Report your result to an accuracy of at least <m>0.000001</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>f($a + $k) \approx T_4($a + $k) = </m><answer name="fEst" allowedErrorInNumbers="0.000001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>$$T4($h)</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        Finally, use a computational device and enter <q><m>$p \sin($a + $k)</m></q>.  What is the absolute error between your estimate from <m>T_4($a + $k)</m> and the value that the computational device reports?  Report the error to an accuracy of at least <m>0.00000001</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>|$p \sin($a + $k) - T_4($a + $k)| = </m><answer name="Err" allowedErrorInNumbers="0.00000001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>abs($$f($h)-$$T4($h))</answer>
      </p>
      
    </li>
  </ol>
</p>